---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { FORMAT_REFERENCE, PRIMARY_FORMATS, type FormatReference } from '../content/format-reference';
const formats = PRIMARY_FORMATS
  .map((id) => FORMAT_REFERENCE[id])
  .filter((format): format is FormatReference => Boolean(format));
---
<SiteLayout title="Supported File Formats | ConvertAnyFile" description="Explore supported image, archive, and structured-data formats in ConvertAnyFile, including ZIP, 7Z, RAR, TAR, TAR.GZ, GZIP, CSV, JSON, JPG, PNG, WebP, HEIC and more.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><span>Formats</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Format reference</span><h1>Choose the right format for the job.</h1><p>ConvertAnyFile has three live browser-first categories: Images, Archives, and structured Data. Each category has its own validation, capability rules, safety limits, and certified outputs.</p></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="content-intro-grid"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Start with the destination</span><h2>Format choice is about the next workflow, not just the extension.</h2><p>A photograph, transparent logo, phone original, archive package, CSV export, API payload, line-oriented dataset, and HDR frame have different requirements. Choose an output based on what the next application accepts and what structure or fidelity must survive the conversion.</p></div><div class="content-summary-card"><strong>How support works</strong><p>Each category has its own validation and output contract. The shared platform does not assume that a file is safe or meaningful just because its filename carries a known extension.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Archives</span><h2>ZIP, 7Z, RAR, TAR, TAR.GZ/TGZ, and GZIP.</h2><p>The Archive category can read common archive/container families locally and repack supported regular files and folders. 7Z and supported RAR4/RAR5 are input-only through a lazy-loaded WebAssembly decoder. Certified public outputs are ZIP, TAR, and TAR.GZ.</p></div><div class="comparison-table-wrap"><table class="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Role</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>ZIP</strong></td><td>Widely compatible multi-file archive</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>7Z</strong></td><td>Compressed archive container</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>RAR</strong></td><td>RAR4/RAR5 archive input</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>TAR</strong></td><td>Unix-style archive container</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>TAR.GZ / TGZ</strong></td><td>Gzip-compressed TAR</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>GZIP / GZ</strong></td><td>Single-file compression stream</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="/archive-converter/">Open Archive Converter <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/rar-to-zip/">RAR to ZIP <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/7z-to-zip/">7Z to ZIP <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/tar-gz-to-zip/">TAR.GZ to ZIP <span>→</span></a></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Structured data</span><h2>CSV, TSV, JSON, JSONL, and NDJSON.</h2><p>The Data category accepts five extensions and produces four output families. NDJSON is treated as JSON Lines input because both formats store one complete JSON value per non-empty line.</p></div><div class="comparison-table-wrap"><table class="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Role</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>CSV</strong></td><td>Comma-delimited tables</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>TSV</strong></td><td>Tab-delimited tables</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>JSON</strong></td><td>Structured objects, arrays, and values</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>JSONL</strong></td><td>One JSON value per line</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NDJSON</strong></td><td>JSON Lines alias/input extension</td><td>Yes</td><td>As JSONL</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="/data-converter/">Open Data Converter <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/csv-to-json/">CSV to JSON <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/json-to-csv/">JSON to CSV <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/jsonl-to-json/">JSONL to JSON <span>→</span></a></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Certified image outputs</span><h2>{formats.length} image output formats in the production catalog.</h2><p>Every image format below is filtered against the WASM runtime running in the browser. If a deployed build cannot write a codec, the converter hides that option rather than offering a conversion that cannot complete.</p></div><div class="format-grid">{formats.map((format)=><article class="format-card" id={format.id}><span>{format.name}</span><h3>{format.role}</h3><p>{format.summary}</p><p><strong>Best for:</strong> {format.bestFor}</p><p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> {format.tradeoffs}</p></article>)}</div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Image comparison</span><h2>Compression, transparency, and compatibility.</h2><p>No single column decides the winner. The right image output depends on the content and destination.</p></div><div class="comparison-table-wrap"><table class="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Compression</th><th>Transparency</th><th>Compatibility note</th></tr></thead><tbody>{formats.map((format)=><tr><td><strong>{format.name}</strong></td><td>{format.compression}</td><td>{format.transparency}</td><td>{format.compatibility}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="content-intro-grid"><div><span class="section-kicker">Different fidelity contracts</span><h2>Pixels, records, and archive trees need different rules.</h2><p>Image conversion creates one output image per source and documents flattening behavior for multi-frame or layered inputs. Data conversion uses explicit table/JSON rules. Archive conversion preserves safe regular file trees where representable while rejecting dangerous paths, password-protected inputs, unsupported special entries, and oversized expansion.</p></div><div class="content-summary-card"><strong>Local first</strong><p>All three live categories use the shared browser-first batch system, but their engines, validation, limits, and output controls remain category-specific.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="/image-converter/">Open Image Converter <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/data-converter/">Open Data Converter <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/archive-converter/">Open Archive Converter <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/guides/zip-vs-tar/">ZIP vs TAR guide <span>→</span></a></div></div></section>
</SiteLayout>
